\description{
Run a Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR) uncertainty step against a
fit that was produced earlier - useful when the original fit was
expensive and you want to add SIR without re-estimating, or when working
with a fit loaded from a \code{.fitrx} bundle.
}
\details{
\code{ferx_sir()} re-uses the fit's asymptotic covariance matrix as the SIR
proposal distribution and the per-subject empirical Bayes ETAs as
warm-starts for the inner loop. The returned fit is the input with
\code{sir_ess}, \code{sir_ci_theta}, \code{sir_ci_omega}, \code{sir_ci_sigma}, \code{sir_ci_kappa}
(IOV models only) and, when \code{sir_keep_samples = TRUE}, \code{sir_resamples} /
\code{sir_resamples_n} / \code{sir_resamples_dim} populated.

The fit is rebuilt at its fitted estimates, including the IOV (kappa)
covariance \code{fit$omega_iov}, under the inner loop of its last estimation
method (FOCE or FOCEI).
\subsection{Settings}{

A fit made with \code{ferx_fit(..., sir = TRUE)}, or returned by an earlier
\code{ferx_sir()}, records the settings its SIR ran under as \code{fit$sir_settings}:
the sample sizes, the seed, the proposal degrees of freedom (\code{df}), the
\code{scale}, \code{keep_samples}, and under \code{scoring} the inner-loop and ODE settings
each draw was scored with. Every argument left at \code{NULL} takes the recorded
value, so \code{ferx_sir(fit)} repeats the fit's SIR: its intervals and effective
sample size are \code{identical()} to the in-fit ones, also after a
\code{\link[=ferx_save_fit]{ferx_save_fit()}} / \code{\link[=ferx_load_fit]{ferx_load_fit()}} round trip. The exception is a
normal proposal (\code{sir_df = Inf}): a \code{.fitrx} bundle cannot record it yet
(ferx-core#1819), so \code{ferx_save_fit()} warns and saves no SIR settings, and
the loaded fit runs as one with no record. An argument you pass
replaces that one setting and keeps the rest. The returned fit's
\code{sir_settings} records the run it made.

A fit with no SIR record (SIR never ran on it, or it was saved before the
record existed) runs with the engine's defaults for every argument left at
\code{NULL}: 1000 samples, 250 resamples, seed 12345, \code{sir_df = 5},
\code{sir_scale = "packed"}, and default inner-loop and ODE settings
(tolerances written in the model file's \verb{[fit_options]} are kept). On such
a fit, settings passed to \code{ferx_fit()} through \verb{settings =} (an
\code{inner_maxiter}, an \code{ode_reltol}) are not used: the engine reads them only
from a SIR record (ferx-core #1806).
}

\subsection{Data selection}{

The data are re-read with the selection the fit was made with, recorded as
\code{fit$reader_settings}: a \code{ferx_fit(ignore =, accept =, ignore_ids =)}
selection applies as well as the model file's \verb{[data_selection]}, and the
engine checks the rows it reads against \code{fit$population_fingerprint}. A fit
made before these were recorded is refused when its \code{fit$exclusions} show a
clause, or its \code{fit$call_settings} an \code{iov_column}, that the model file does
not state, rather than scored on other data.
}

\subsection{Integrity check}{

\code{ferx_fit()} records the model and data file paths plus SHA-256 hashes
on the returned fit (\code{fit$model_path}, \code{fit$data_path},
\code{fit$model_hash}, \code{fit$data_hash}). \code{ferx_sir()} re-reads those files
and verifies the hashes; if either file changed since the fit, the call
is a \strong{hard error}. The point of running SIR against the original fit
is to refine its uncertainty estimate, which is meaningless against a
modified model or dataset.

Edge case: if hashing failed at fit time (e.g. permission flip between
parse and hash) the corresponding \verb{fit$*_hash} is \code{NA} and the
integrity check silently passes on that side. This is rare in
practice - hashing would have to fail while the parse just
succeeded - but if you require integrity verification, check
\code{!is.na(fit$model_hash) && !is.na(fit$data_hash)} before relying on
the protection.
